{"id":"f35785cf-ab01-4e66-978d-7e2f1d322d40","object":"chat.completion","created":1784998561,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\\n    all_ids = set(node_map.keys())\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed = []\\n    retained = set(all_ids)\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        temp_retained = retained - {nid}\\n        all_symbols_ok = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            if not any(node_map[nid2]['symbol'] == sym for nid2 in temp_retained):\\n                all_symbols_ok = False\\n                break\\n        if all_symbols_ok:\\n            removed.append(nid)\\n            retained = temp_retained\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': sorted(retained)}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that processes the given payload. The payload contains nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. The task is to perform grammar-guided node reduction while preserving interestingness (presumably that all required symbols remain represented). The output should be removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nInterpretation: We have a tree of nodes. We need to decide which nodes to remove based on some criteria. The problem statement: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" through solve(case) for a bounded objective: Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle. But the details are vague. However, the fixture payloads show nodes with removable flag. Also required_symbols. The task likely is: given a tree with nodes that may be removable or not, and required symbols that must be present, determine which nodes can be removed while keeping the required symbols. Possibly we need to remove nodes greedily in some order (deeper nodes before shallower, tie break by ID) if they are removable and their removal does not cause any required symbol to be lost? Also need to consider that if a node is removed, its children might be orphaned? The nodes have parent references, but no children list. We need to infer children.\n\nThe problem mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". But we don't have a grammar. Instead we have symbols and required_symbols. Perhaps the \"interestingness oracle\" is that all required symbols must remain represented in the tree after removal. Also node removal might be allowed only if the node is removable. Also we must \"never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That is a constraint.\n\nSo algorithm: Start with all nodes. Determine which nodes are \"retained\" initially (all nodes). Then we iterate over nodes in some order (depth descending, then tie break by ID ascending? Actually \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So process nodes sorted by depth descending, then by ID (maybe ascending for tie break? The problem says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\"). For each node, if it is not the root, not a required symbol (but required_symbols are symbols, not node IDs. So we need to check if the node's symbol is in required_symbols? But careful: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So \"required symbol\" means any node whose symbol is in required_symbols? Or does it mean nodes that are required (maybe the node itself is required if its symbol is required?). In the fixtures, root has symbol \"root\" which is in required_symbols. Also n1 has symbol \"s1\" which is also required. So they are non-removable (removable=false). So the rule may be: a node is not removable if its symbol is in required_symbols, or if it is the root.\n\nBut also: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So after removal, we must still have at least one node with each required symbol.\n\nAlso: \"a node with retained children\" - if any child of this node is retained, we cannot remove it. So we must check children.\n\nSo process: For each node (in order), if it is removable and its symbol is not in required_symbols (or maybe root is special but root's removable=false anyway), and if after removing it (and potentially its subtree? The node's children might become retained or be removed? Actually if we remove a node, its children are orphaned. But the tree structure might require that children are attached to parent? In parse trees, deleting an internal node may require reattaching its children to its parent. The problem mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". Possibly we need to consider that when removing a node, its children become children of the node's parent? But the fixture doesn't provide a grammar to validate. Maybe the simple interpretation: a node can be removed only if all its children are also removable? Or if removing it does not break the tree structure? The statement \"a node with retained children\" suggests that if any child is retained (not removed), then the node cannot be removed because that would orphan the child. So we can only remove a node if all its children are also removed (or it has no children). But then we need to process in post-order (children first) to ensure that we only remove nodes whose children have already been removed or are also being removed. That matches \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process depth descending, so children (deeper) are considered before parent. So if a child is retained (not removed), then the parent cannot be removed because it has retained children. So the rule: a node can be removed if it is removable, its symbol is not required, and all its children have been removed (or it has no children). Additionally, after removal, all required symbols must still be present in the remaining nodes. But if a child is removed, then the required symbol from that child might be lost. So we need to ensure that the required symbols are covered by other nodes.\n\nThus algorithm: Start with all nodes. Mark all nodes as retained initially? Or we decide removal order. We'll maintain a set of removed node IDs. We'll also maintain a set of retained node IDs (initially all). Then we iterate nodes in order (depth descending, tie by ID ascending). For each node, if it is removable (removable flag true) and its symbol is not in required_symbols (i.e., not required), and if all its children (nodes whose parent is this node) are already removed (i.e., not in retained set), and if after removing this node, every required symbol is still present in the retained set (i.e., there exists at least one node with that symbol that is still retained), then we remove it: add to removed_node_ids, remove from retained set. Otherwise, we keep it. At the end, the retained set is all nodes not removed. Order of removal is the order we processed (since we only remove when condition holds). Then output sorted retained IDs and removal order (the order we removed them). The output requires sorted list for retained_node_ids and removed_node_ids in order of removal (the problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed. retained_node_ids sorted.\n\nWe must also consider that root is not removable (removable=false). So it will stay.\n\nCheck the first fixture: nodes:\nn2 removable true, symbol s2 not required, children: n4 (removable true). n3 not removable, children n5. n1 not removable, children n6 (removable true). Depth order: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. Then depth1: n1,n2,n3. Then depth0 root.\nProcessing depth2 first: n4 (removable true, symbol s1 not required? s1 is required! So n4's symbol is s1, which is in required_symbols. So cannot remove because required symbol. So n4 retained. n5 symbol s2 not required, but n5's removable is false, so cannot remove. n6 symbol s0 not required, removable true, but children? n6 has no children (depth2, no nodes have parent n6). So we can consider. But we need to check required symbols after removal. Required symbols: root and s1. After removing n6, still have root (root symbol), and s1 from n1 or n4? n1 has symbol s1, n4 has symbol s1. So s1 remains. So n6 can be removed. So remove n6. Then depth1: n1 (removable false), n2 (removable true, symbol s2 not required, children: n4 is still retained (we didn't remove n4). So n2 has retained child n4, so cannot remove. n3 (removable false). So only n6 removed. So removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"], retained_node_ids = sorted(all except n6) = [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? But root ID is \"root\". So sorted: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]. That seems plausible.\n\nCheck second fixture: similar structure. n4 removed? n4 symbol s1 required, so cannot. n6 removable true, symbol s0, not required. So remove n6. Then no other removable. So same result.\n\nThird: same.\n\nBut wait: In first fixture, n2 is removable and its symbol is not required, but it has retained child n4 (since n4 not removed due to required symbol). So n2 cannot be removed. That matches.\n\nThus algorithm seems correct.\n\nEdge cases: What if a node is removable and its child is also removable, but the child's removal would remove a required symbol? Then we should not remove the child (because required symbol), so child is retained, then parent cannot be removed. That's fine.\n\nWe must also handle that nodes can have multiple children. The fixture has each node with one or zero children? Actually n2 has child n4, n3 has n5, n1 has n6. Root has three children. If a parent has multiple children, we need all children to be removed for parent to be removable. That matches.\n\nBut note: The problem says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That implies we should check after removal that the set of symbols from retained nodes still covers all required_symbols. But if we only remove a node that has no children (leaf), its symbol might be unique for that required symbol? The check would prevent removal. So that's handled.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Parse case payload: contains nodes list and required_symbols list.\n2. Build a dict mapping node id to node object. Also build a mapping from parent id to list of child ids (children). We'll need children list for each node to check.\n3. Identify all node ids.\n4. Create set of required_symbols.\n5. Determine processing order: sort nodes by depth descending, then by id (string) ascending. Depth is given in node.\n6. Initialize removed_ids = [] (list in order), retained_ids = set(all node ids).\n7. For each node in sorted order:\n   - if node id not in retained_ids: skip (already removed? shouldn't happen because we process in order, but if parent removed later, children processed earlier. But we only remove if condition holds, and we process deeper first, so if we remove a child, its parent is processed later. That's fine. If a child is removed, it's already removed when we process parent.\n   - Check if node is removable: node['removable'] must be True.\n   - Check if node's symbol is not in required_symbols. If it is, skip.\n   - Check if node