the implementation as a JSON string under key \"implementation\". So we need to produce a Python source code string. That source code defines the solve function.\n\nWe need to ensure that the function does not mutate case, but we can read its fields.\n\nWrite the Python code accordingly.\n\nWe should include comments? Not necessary, but okay.\n\nWe need to escape the source for JSON: newlines become \\n, backslashes become \\\\, quotes become \\\".\n\nI'll write the implementation as a triple-quoted string inside the JSON, but careful: JSON string cannot contain unescaped control characters. I will use raw string in Python but then need to escape when embedding. Alternatively, I can generate the JSON manually. Since it's a simple implementation, I'll write it directly as a JSON string with appropriate escaping.\n\nI'll produce:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(n['parent'], []).append(n['id'])\\n    all_ids = set(node_map.keys())\\n    # sort by depth descending, then by id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed = []\\n    retained = set(all_ids)\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if node['parent'] is None:  # root\\n            continue\\n        # check children all removed\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        # check required symbols survive removal\\n        # simulate removal\\n        temp_retained = retained - {nid}\\n        # check each symbol still present\\n        all_symbols_ok = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            if not any(node_map[nid2]['symbol'] == sym for nid2 in temp_retained):\\n                all_symbols_ok = False\\n                break\\n        if all_symbols_ok:\\n            removed.append(nid)\\n            retained = temp_retained\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed,\\n        'retained_node_ids': sorted(retained)\\n    }\"\n}\n\nWait, I set default for children dict, but I used setdefault incorrectly. I'll fix: children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes} then loop: if n['parent']: children[n['parent']].append(n['id']). That's fine.\n\nAlso, the check for retained_ids includes nodes that might have been removed? We only iterate over nodes in sorted order, but if a node is removed, it will be removed from retained. Later nodes (shallower) that are children of this removed node? No, children are deeper, processed earlier. So when we process a parent, its children are already processed and possibly removed. The condition checks if any child is still retained (in retained set). That works.\n\nAlso, we need to ensure that the retained set initially is all_ids. But we may remove nodes, but we only remove if conditions hold. That's fine.\n\nNow, we must ensure that we don't mutate case. We are not.\n\nNow, produce the final JSON.\n\nI'll write it carefully, escaping:\n\n- Newlines: \\n\n- Quotes inside: \\\"\n- Backslashes: \\\\ (none except in \\n)\n\nLet me write the source code as a string, then escape.\n\nAlternatively, I can use Python to generate the JSON string, but that's not possible here. I'll do it manually.\n\nThe source code (if I write it as multiline string) but for JSON it must be a single line. So I'll escape newlines.\n\nI'll write:\n\ndef solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\'nodes\\']\\n    required_symbols = set(case[\\'required_symbols\\'])\\n    node_map = {n[\\'id\\']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n[\\'id\\']: [] for n in nodes}