uses single quotes inside the Python string. In the JSON string, we write n['id'] as n['id']? That would be interpreted as n['id'] in JSON? Actually the JSON string is just a sequence of characters. We need to include the backslash? No, the source code uses single quotes, so the character sequence is n['id'] which includes single quotes. In JSON, single quotes are allowed as characters, no need to escape. So it's fine.\n\nI'll also replace the line breaks with \\n. I'll produce a single line string.\n\nNow, let me write the final JSON output.\n\nI'll enclose the whole response in a JSON object. The response must be exactly one valid JSON object with the single key \"implementation\". So:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\\n    all_ids = set(node_map.keys())\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed = []\\n    retained = set(all_ids)\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        temp_retained = retained - {nid}\\n        all_symbols_ok = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            if not any(node_map[nid2]['symbol'] == sym for nid2 in temp_retained):\\n                all_symbols_ok = False\\n                break\\n        if all_symbols_ok:\\n            removed.append(nid)\\n            retained = temp_retained\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': sorted(retained)}\"}\n\nI need to ensure that the string is properly escaped for JSON. In the string above, I have single quotes inside, which are fine. But I also have double quotes? No. The only double quotes are the outer JSON delimiter. So it's valid.\n\nHowever, there is a potential issue: The string contains backslashes for \\n. Those are valid in JSON string as escape sequences. But what about any backslash in the source code? None. So it's fine.\n\nI'll now produce the final response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2054,"completion_tokens":5185,"total_tokens":7239,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4828},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":6},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}